 during the era of the final Tirthankara, Lord Mahavir, when he was 37 years old, and even then the image was already years old. This impressive image gained fame and grandeur day by day. Over time this influential and popular temple fell into ruin, and the presiding deities of the image, Dharanendra and Padmavati, carried this image of Lord Parshwanath away to the heavenly realm (Devalok). This image was worshipped by gods and goddesses for a long time, and its glorious history became even more magnificent in later years. Shri Gajsingh was a follower of the Jain faith. Pleased by his devotion, the goddess Padmavati gave this magnificent image of Lord Parshwanath to the shravaka Gajsingh. His joy knew no bounds, and he became even more absorbed in his prayers. He built 108 beautiful and grand Jain temples (jinalayas) and installed this image of Lord Parshwanath as the Main Deity. Over time this temple was lost, but this impressive and miraculous image of Lord Parshwanath remained buried beneath the earth. There was a Thakur named Bhuvan Singh of Ugna village, who had a well dug. When it was dug to a depth of 44 feet, it filled with milk. When further digging was done, together with the serpent-king (Dharanendra), to uncover the secret of this miracle, this image of Lord Parshwanath was found. The landowner Bhuvansingh and the villagers handed this image over to the Jain sangh. The Jain community decided to build a magnificent temple in Ugna village and install this image in it. When the temple's construction was nearing completion, seven Jain shravakas had the same dream on one night: the presiding deity of the image indicated that Shri Rawatmalji and others of Durg, Madhya Pradesh, wished to build a grand structure, and that this image should be installed in the temple at Nagpura, near Durg. Shri Rawatmalji was contacted and informed of this. On the advice of Acharya Shri Kailashsagarsurishwarji, the image of Lord Parshwanath was brought to Nagpura. On an auspicious day, a new image of Lord Parshwanath was installed at the Ugna temple. When the foundation of this temple was being laid in the presence of monks and Jain devotees, a stream of milk flowed forth. This temple was gifted 40 other images. A grand and beautiful temple with three domes was built and the images installed. Many miracles continue to occur here even today.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"article\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"108 Parshwanath, No. 101 – Shri Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: Post – Nagpur (Nagpura), District – Durg (Chhattisgarh).\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"2\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Main Deity: A black image of Lord Shri Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath in the padmasana posture, 47 inches (about 118 cm) tall. Above the head of the image is a canopy of 7 serpent hoods. Below and behind this image, the serpent-king (Nagraj) can be seen in a coiled padmasana posture — an image unique of its kind.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"3\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: This is in Nagpura village, on the western bank of the Shivnath river.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"4\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$23\\\"}],\\\"$L24\\\",\\\"$L25\\\",\\\"$L26\\\",\\\"$L27\\\",\\\"$L28\\\",\\\"$L29\\\",\\\"$L2a\\\",\\\"$L2b\\\",\\\"$L2c\\\",\\\"$L2d\\\"]}]\\n1e:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"nav\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-123\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":[[\\\"path\\\",{\\\"d\\\":\\\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\\\",\\\"key\\\":\\\"1wnfg3\\\"}]],\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Sammetshikhar Parshwanath\\\"}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-125\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Kesariya Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":[[\\\"path\\\",{\\\"d\\\":\\\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\\\",\\\"key\\\":\\\"mthhwq\\\"}]],\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}]]}]]}]\\n1f:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"footer\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:justify-between\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"children\\\":\\\"१०८ पार्श्वनाथ तीर्थ दर्शन — श्री पार्श्वनाथ भगवान के तीर्थों का डिजिटल संकलन\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Original source: Rajendra Kumar Jain, Parshv Sadan, Parshv Nagar, Dungarpur\\\"}]]}]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"24:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"5\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Because this image removes the mental, physical, and verbal troubles of devotees, and because worship and prayer here dispel such troubles, this powerful image of the Lord is called Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath. It is said that once a non-Jain person, suffering from cancer, was on his way to Bombay for treatment. Coming to Nagpura with faith, trust, and firm confidence in Lord Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath, he worshipped the Lord, and then went to Tata Hospital in Bombay for his treatment. When the report came back after examination, everyone was astonished — there was no cancer. Because of his faith in the Lord, he had been cured of his cancer.\\\"}]\\n25:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"6\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Other Temples: There is no other temple here.\\\"}]\\n26:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"7\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Art and Sculpture: The image, with its seven serpent hoods, looks beautiful; this wondrous and miraculous image impresses devotees and inspires them to place their faith in the Lord. The main attraction of this temple is its 30-foot-high gateway, above which is an image of Lord Parshwanath.\\\"}]\\n2e:T438,The Shri Uvsaggaharam Parshwa Tirtha of Nagpura has become a symbol of deep faith for devotees throughout the world. In keeping with devotees' wish that this shrine — a place of holy austerities and blessings (mangal kalyan avasa) — also offer them darshan and inspiration from other pilgrimage sites, a Tirth Darshan Deheri has been built as part of the shrine's comprehensive development. In the garden's shrines (deheri), natural alcoves have been built with artistic plaques and images of 25 pilgrimage lords, including Shri Shankheshwar, Girnar, Kadambgiri, Talaja, Abu, Osianji, and Pavapuri, under the sacred guidance of the eminent scholar-monk, revered Acharya Bhagwant Shrimad Sushil Surishwar Maharajsaheb, and the powerful and eloquent preacher, revered Acharyadev Shrimad Jinottam Surishwarji Maharajsaheb. Ancient images of Shri Anantnath, Shri Sumatinath, and Lord Mahavir Swami are enshrined in the small shrines (devkulika) of this garden; at its centre, a magnificent artistic Shri Shashvata Jin Mandir and a Shri Rajendra Guru Mandir are under construction.27:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"8\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$2e\\\"}]\\n28:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"9\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"The Manibhadravir temple, the Padmavati temple, and other temples are also found here. The Manibhadravir here is very powerful and miraculous. This historic and ancient shrine, which had fallen into ruin for want of grand restoration, was taken up for renovation in 1979 by the devout Ravalmal Jain 'Mani' of the Dugad clan, a resident of Durg, following instructions received in a dream, and today, with its grand seven-spired temple complex, the greatly powerful Shri Uvsaggaharam Parshwa Tirtha shines resplendent. In Samvat 2059 (1995), under the guidance of revered Acharya Shri Rajyash Suri Maharajsaheb, a beloved disciple of Pujya Atmakamal Labdhi-Vikram Guru, this grand construction was completed, and under his sacred guidance the consecration of the Shri Uvsaggaharam Parshwa Jinalaya took place.\\\"}]\\n29:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"10\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Directions: The nearest railway station is Durg (Junction), on the Nagpur–Howrah railway line. Nagpura Tirtha is 14 km from Durg. Bus service and private vehicles are available from Durg to Nagpura Tirtha. Dharamshala and bhojanshala facilities are available here. Train no. 12833 Ahmedabad–Howrah Express is available from Ahmedabad to Durg.\\\"}]\\n2a:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"11\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Scripture References: The antiquity of this image is mentioned in the book \\\\\\\"History of Chhattisgarh's Dynasties and Culture – Capital Cities.\\\\\\\" The main temple of Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath is in Nagpura. There is an image of Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath at Kareda (Bhupalsagar, Rajasthan) and at Jiravala Tirtha. There is also a temple of Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath at Warangal, Andhra Pradesh, with a beautiful and attractive image of Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath in the padmasana posture, about 68 cm tall, with a canopy of 7 serpent hoods above the head. Shri Jain Shwetambar Mandir, JP Anee Road, Warangal – 506002, Andhra Pradesh.\\\"}]\\n2b:[\\\"$\\\"\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"12\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Hymn of Praise: By the teachings of Keshi Swami, King Pradeshi consecrated your image; you appeared together with Dharanendra amid a stream of milk; you who endure hardship, you who remove the hardships of all. \\\\\\\"With loving devotion I bow in reverence to Shri Uvsaggaharam Parshwa.\\\\\\\"\\\"}]\\n2c:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"13\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Chanting Mantra: \\\\\\\"Om Hreem Arham Shri Uvsaggaharam Parshwanathaya Namah\\\\\\\"\\\"}]\\n2d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"14\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Trust: Shri Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Post: Nagpura, District: Durg, State: Chhattisgarh – 491001. Phone: 0788-2621201/2621202.\\\"}]\\n\"])</script></body></html>","rscData":"","segmentData":{"/_tree":"","/_full":"","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug/__PAGE__":"","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug":"","/$d$lang/tirtha":"","/$d$lang":"","/_index":"","/_head":""},"headers":{"x-nextjs-stale-time":"300","x-nextjs-prerender":"1","x-next-cache-tags":"_N_T_/layout,_N_T_/[lang]/layout,_N_T_/[lang]/tirtha/layout,_N_T_/[lang]/tirtha/[slug]/layout,_N_T_/[lang]/tirtha/[slug]/page,_N_T_/en/tirtha/tirtha-124"},"segmentPaths":["/_tree","/_full","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug/__PAGE__","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug","/$d$lang/tirtha","/$d$lang","/_index","/_head"],"revalidate":60,"cacheControl":{"revalidate":60,"expire":31536000}}}